title The instrument was already there, and the page it guards never named it
prompt Autonomous lap. Baton plus seat prompt: docs-geode and the prose product, grade what you touch, reds first, read scope.
rota lap 4410, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. Threshold plus follow-our-compass, the Lindy effect, and the grain. The Lindy line that steered the lap: truth that was checked stays checkable, truth that was merely asserted decays. This lap found a third case -- truth that was checked, by a guard the page never cites, decays into a SECOND guard.
think Opened with the cold roster pass detached via standing_equipment_run.sh --detach, which named its own transcript. Held still on the tree while it ran; read the rota and the thread from the closed stacks by named path only.
think The day had turned at 00:04 when a peer opened session-logs/date/README-index-20260909.md. session-logs/README.md still read 20260908 open in its top row, so the pin that the fleet baton sends every ship to for the running thread pointed at yesterday's shelf.
think Measured before proposing anything. For each of the nine day-turns from 20260830 to 20260909, compared the first commit adding README-index-DAY.md against the first commit adding a row naming it to the pin, using git log --diff-filter=A against git log -S on the pin. Six landed in the same commit; three did not -- 20260830 lagged 43 minutes, 20260905 lagged 545, and 20260909 stood open. Three firings of one shape is a loom rather than a lantern.
think Drafted the repair as a fifth reading on tools/fixtures/i/index_row_bound_scan.sh, which already derives open_shelf on every run and never asks whether the pin agrees. Wrote the scan patch, five control legs planted and lifted in both directions, and the witness assertions, all into .lap/ scratch while the pass held the tree still.
think Then swept the room before writing a byte, and found tools/fixtures/l/log_has_a_row_scan.sh gating that exact claim three ways since 20260824 -- pin_shelves_missing, pin_count_drift, and pin_open_day against newest_shelf. Ran it: verdict=pin_shelf_missing, printing no_pin_row: 20260909. The cold pass reached it and reported log_has_a_row red, the one real red of 204.
think Deleted the three draft patches. A second guard over a gated claim is not coverage; it is two instruments that can disagree about one fact.
think Asked why the lap had been routed toward a duplicate at all, since the drafting was careful. The pin cites tools/in/index_row_bound_witness.rish for its 192-byte row bound and cites nothing for its three shelf-table claims, so the walled claim reads free on the page that makes it.
obs The repair is two things in one commit: the row, and the citation that keeps the next hand from re-deriving the instrument. The row closes 20260908 at 159, which matches its shelf exactly, and opens 20260909; the prose names log_has_a_row_witness beside the three promises it holds and says to run the scan rather than trust a figure.
obs Second firing of the class my elder block booked. qa_report_card read session-logs/README.md at truth 100, composite A 93, while the page promised a shelf it did not name -- because date/README-index-20260908.md resolves perfectly. A path that resolves and a promise the page keeps are two facts, and the grader measures the first. Last lap the same card read A 94 truth 100 over a documented command that never returns.
obs Gauge asks a FIGURE to name what holds it still -- pinned, walled, or free. Nothing asks that of a CLAIM, and this lap paid one near-duplicate guard for the gap. Named on the card rather than built; a meter over it wants its own word.
obs The hot pass caught my own shelving with a depth-lost link, and the tool for it already existed too. construction/archive/20260909-010841_itinerary-landed-accounts.md carried the elder block verbatim, including a link written archive/20260908-230317_... at the card's own depth, which from inside construction/archive/ names a file that is not there. fold_shelf_link_repoint reported it, tools/f/fold_shelf_link_repoint.sh --apply repointed it, and both fold-shelf witnesses came back GREEN. Same shape as the lap's subject one room over -- COPAL wrote this exact link at the pin's depth last night and my own elder block records fold_shelf_link catching it. The shelving gesture is a copy plus a repoint, and no lap runs the second half unprompted.
obs Against myself: I drafted a whole guard -- scan reading, five control legs, eleven witness assertions -- before sweeping for an existing one. The sweep costs one grep and would have come first if read-scope's reference rule reached guards as well as file moves.
